WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 0.2 · Start Here: Your Founder Journey
Your founding profile

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
One page of plain facts about what you are building, which people read first.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.133
A founding profile is one page of plain facts about what you are building. It is not a pitch: it is the page people read first to decide whether, and how, they can work with you.

4
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:29.133
What goes on the page

5
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:42.315
Name and sentence: Your registered or working name, and what you will do, for whom and where. For example: “We will bake and sell bread, and train young people who left school early, in one neighbourhood.”

6
00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:55.867
Form and stage: The legal form you intend, how far along you are and who is involved so far. For example: A company with a social purpose, not yet registered; pilot stage; two co-founders, four trainees planned.

7
00:00:55.867 --> 00:01:10.530
Activities and places: What you plan to do, with whom and where. Flag children, personal data or public money. For example: A Saturday market stall and a shared kitchen; trainees aged 17 to 24, so we hold young people’s data.

8
00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:21.948
Still to decide: Open decisions, written as questions rather than guesses. For example: Should we register now, or stay informal for the first three months of the pilot?

9
00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:22.948
Why it matters

10
00:01:22.948 --> 00:01:28.622
1. You will give these facts in applications, forms and first conversations.

11
00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:34.296
2. Your legal form shapes control, surplus, fundraising and who regulates you.

12
00:01:34.296 --> 00:01:40.230
3. Your stage decides how much formality you need now.

13
00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:41.230
Where you will reuse it

14
00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:45.422
1. Funding applications, bank accounts and registration forms.

15
00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:49.615
2. The About page of your first website.

16
00:01:49.615 --> 00:01:56.289
3. Your choice of legal form and, later, registration and legal duties.

17
00:01:56.689 --> 00:01:57.289
What to avoid

18
00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:02.963
Writing a pitch or a mission statement instead of plain facts.

19
00:02:02.963 --> 00:02:07.156
Guessing answers you have not decided yet.

20
00:02:07.156 --> 00:02:14.200
Registering only because a template, a friend or a funder suggests it.

21
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.600
You write your founding profile in six short parts.

22
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.600
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.200
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
